WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.560
Is this introduction weird?

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:02.880
Yes, it's.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.380
If "it's" is just a contraction
of "it" and "is",

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:08.820
then why doesn't that work?

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.920
In fact, why didn't English
just do a find-and-replace

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:12.120
on all the contractions?

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.920
I'm sure there'dn't've been
any problems with that.

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:18.120
Ah. Well, how do we understand
where the problem's with this?

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.340
I was supposed to've'd a
brilliant idea here,

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:21.300
but it appears...

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:22.560
it'sn't.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.600
Okay, let's expand those back out.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.400
That took me about
seventeen takes to get right.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.760
Why don't those
contractions work?

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:32.280
Let's talk about "it's" in that
introductory line.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.320
"It" is just a pronoun,
doing its job,

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.300
referring to something that's
established earlier

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.420
or obvious from context.
But what's the "'s"?

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.340
You can't even really
say it on its own.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:44.100
It's just kind-of leaning on
the "it" for substance.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.740
Which is why the technical term
for it is "clitic",

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:49.920
from the Ancient Greek "enklitikos",
meaning "to lean on".

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.360
A clitic is a part of speech that
can't exist just on its own.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.220
Now, we've talked about things
like this before:

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.500
prefixes, suffixes and
other affixes,

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:01.800
things that attach to words
like ‘-able' or ‘un-'.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.660
But a clitic isn't one of those.

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:06.780
And we know that because it doesn
t attach to individual words:

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:10.140
it attaches to a phrase,
a bigger part of speech.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.300
So using a prefix or affix,

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.400
you can go from a dog to a

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.100
proto-dog or an anti-dog
or an astro-dog.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.020
Or you can say that something
is "dog-ish".

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:21.660
Those all attach to the word "dog".

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:25.200
If you add some detail, you say
"my beautiful, green, astro-dog",

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.620
not "my astro-beautiful, green, dog".
That doesn't work.

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.720
For a clitic, it's different.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:33.000
"My astro-dog's been learning tricks."

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:34.500
There's the clitic, just after "dog".

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:35.880
Let's add some more detail.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:39.300
"My astro-dog from Jupiter's
been learning tricks."

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.880
That movement of 's,
within the sentence,

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:45.480
means it can't be an affix;
the ‘s behaves like it's the whole word,

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:48.060
like it's "has".
Not "is"… "has".

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:52.740
All that means that ‘s has to be
in its own category, it's a clitic.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:55.740
But that doesn't explain why
it doesn't work sometimes.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:59.400
If it was always possible to contract
‘is' and ‘has' down like that,

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:02.700
there might be an argument that
it's just a pronunciation thing?

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:04.800
There has to be some additional reason

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:08.520
that means "it'sn't" and "yes, it's"
are ungrammatical.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:11.760
Now, if you want the full answer
to that, you can read

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.160
Anderson's "Aspects of the
Theory of Clitics",

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:18.840
but a quick warning, it is 287 pages long
not including the references and indices.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:23.880
So to summarize as best as I can
for English: there are two reasons.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:26.700
The first is what's called a Syntactic Gap

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:28.800
which means the presence
of the clitic

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.860
strongly implies that another
word should follow.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:34.320
I think Anderson's best example is:

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:36.720
"Who do you think you're?"

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:41.340
...who do you think you're what?
Meeting? Talking to?

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:43.440
It feels like there
should be another word

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:44.580
in that syntactic gap,

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:46.560
the clitic is "stranded"
at the end.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.600
And flipping that around,
if you say

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.220
"Who do you think
you are meeting?",

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:53.400
the sentence feels…
not ungrammatical,

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.860
but maybe a little
awkward and robotic.

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:58.500
And very much tied to that
is the second reason:

00:02:58.500 --> 00:02:59.820
stress patterns.

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.220
Part of the reason these forms
can be reduced so much

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:04.920
is because they're generally
unstressed, or weak.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.440
They're pronounced quieter
and less distinctly.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:10.740
You cannot stress a clitic, and
it sounds weird when you try.

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:12.000
So let's try!

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.820
If a child's parent is pestering them
because they haven't cleaned up,

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:17.100
the kid can reply
"i AM going to do it",

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:18.660
that's called ‘rejoiner emphasis',

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:21.780
but they can't say
"iMMM going to do it". Doesn't work.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:25.680
So if the verb would contextually
be in its weak form, unstressed,

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:28.020
and it also starts with
/w/, /h/, or a vowel,

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:30.360
it can get even more reduced
to this clitic form.

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:33.480
So, we get "we'll," "they've,"
"you'd," I'm," and so on.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.180
Repeat enough times,
they become grammaticalized,

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:39.060
and they start to get
written like that as well.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:42.600
In theory, something like
"there'd'n't've'" could work.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:46.020
After all, "shouldn't've" is grammatical
in spoken English, as in

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:47.400
"I shouldn't've done that."

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.620
Actually, in my dialect,
if I'm talking quickly,

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:52.620
I pronounce that as either /ʃədənə/
or even just /ʃədn/.

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:54.060
Like, "/ʃədn/ done that".

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:58.500
Languages change and evolve over time,
words become clitics,

00:03:58.500 --> 00:03:59.820
can stay that way for a while

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:03.300
and then eventually they may
start to sound ungrammatical

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:05.040
to future generations.
After all,

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:08.580
we used to say "'tis" not "it's",
so who knows:

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:11.220
in a century or two,
maybe "there'd'n't've"

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:13.140
will be perfectly
unremarkable English.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:16.200
If you want more detail about this,
there's a whole episode

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:19.860
on my co-author Gretchen McCulloch's
podcast "Lingthusiasm",

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:21.900
the link is on screen
or in the description.

